WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.760
INTRO:
For the longest time, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:07.920
two biggest retailers in the world were Amazon and&nbsp;
Walmart. Alibaba was briefly ahead of Walmart over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:13.200
the past few years, but ever since their stock got&nbsp;
crushed, they have fallen back underneath Walmart.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:16.640
I don’t think any of these companies&nbsp;
surprise any of us though as Walmart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:21.760
Amazon, and Alibaba sell everything underneath&nbsp;
the sun. But ironically, none of these retailers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:26.880
are currently the second largest retailer in the&nbsp;
world. That title actually goes to the Home Depot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:33.280
Not only does Home Depot have a higher market cap&nbsp;
than Walmart, but they’re actually pulling in more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:39.520
profit. Over the past 12 months, the Home Depot&nbsp;
profited $21.38 billion while Walmart profited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:45.600
$18.43 billion. Yet, the Home Depot isn’t hyped&nbsp;
up by the media, its founders aren’t criticized to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:51.360
death, and they aren’t constantly accused of being&nbsp;
a monopoly. To most people, Home Depot is just the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:56.640
friendly neighborhood hardware store, and that’s&nbsp;
pretty much it. So, how did such an inconspicuous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:00.560
and relatively niche home improvement retailer&nbsp;
become the world’s largest physical retailer?

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:05.280
ROUGH BEGINNINGS:&nbsp;

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:09.360
Taking a look back, Home Depot is actually&nbsp;
a relatively young retailer being founded in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:15.680
1978 by four men named Bernard Marcus, Arthur&nbsp;
Blank, Ron Brill, and Pat Farrah. At the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:20.000
the booming 60s had come to a close and&nbsp;
the inflation crisis was nearing an end.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.160
Homeowners who had bought their homes during the&nbsp;
economic boom of the 1960s were in a really good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:30.160
position as their homes had skyrocketed in nominal&nbsp;
value due to rampant inflation. So, these guys had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:34.560
plenty of equity in their homes, and as long as&nbsp;
their jobs somewhat kept up with inflation, their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:39.120
monthly mortgage payments had become significantly&nbsp;
more affordable. So, a lot of homeowners were in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:43.600
a position where they could afford to fix up&nbsp;
their houses or even upgrade their houses. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:48.000
unfortunately, the process of improving your home&nbsp;
was super tedious especially if you were trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:52.400
do it by yourself. Homeowners often had to shop&nbsp;
around multiple hardware stores to buy simple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:56.960
tools and if you needed something unique, you&nbsp;
were basically screwed. Home Depot’s founders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:00.960
had of course picked up on this shortfall and&nbsp;
felt that they could address the issue. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:05.280
Bernard and Arthur contacted an investment banker&nbsp;
named Ken Langone to secure some start-up capital.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:08.880
They would use this money to lease&nbsp;
some retail space from JCPenney&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:13.040
and open the first two stores in Atlanta Georgia&nbsp;
called Atlanta’s Do It Yourself WareHouse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:16.800
But despite the quick start, Home&nbsp;
Depot didn’t enjoy quick success.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:20.400
When the store opened its doors, the founders&nbsp;
decided to do a promotional campaign where they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:24.480
would hand out $700 to random people and&nbsp;
invite them to come spend it at the store.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:28.400
But they weren’t even able to hand out all the&nbsp;
money by the end of the first day as they couldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:33.520
convince enough people to try the store. Things&nbsp;
weren’t much better on the supplier side either.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:37.920
Most vendors didn’t want to deal with a new&nbsp;
store without a proven business model. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:41.520
things were so bad that the founders resorted&nbsp;
to stocking the stores with empty boxes and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:46.320
paint cans to make the store look fuller than it&nbsp;
really was. Despite the bleak outlook, it didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:50.960
take long for Home Depot to lock in on their first&nbsp;
differentiating factor which was customer service.

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:54.880
CUSTOMER SERVICE:&nbsp;

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:59.600
From the very beginning Home Depot prioritized&nbsp;
great customer service, but it wasn’t until a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:03.760
few months into running that store that customer&nbsp;
service became a differentiating factor. Bernard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:08.240
recalls that one day early on, a customer came&nbsp;
to the store to return an item. But the customer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:12.080
felt that the employees and Bernard had been&nbsp;
so helpful that they felt bad about returning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:16.880
the item and asking for their money back. So, the&nbsp;
customer brought some okra that they had grown in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:21.360
the garden to give to Bernard during the return.&nbsp;
Of course, the okra didn’t make it any easier to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:25.760
run the business, but it clearly showcased the&nbsp;
importance of happy customers. From then on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:31.120
the Home Depot accepted a strict policy of: the&nbsp;
customer is always right. One of the best examples&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:35.920
of this was when a customer tried to return a&nbsp;
tire at Home Depot. You see, one of Home Depot’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:40.320
first stores was built inside a warehouse&nbsp;
that was originally a tire store. One day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:44.320
a customer came to the store to try returning&nbsp;
a tire they had bought from the old store,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:49.040
and despite not having sold the tire themselves,&nbsp;
Home Depot accepted the return. The tire was then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:53.360
hung on the cashier's stand to remind employees&nbsp;
of the company's core values. Aside from keeping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:58.360
customers happy, Home Depot also prioritized&nbsp;
employee happiness. Bernard and Arthur knew first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:02.880
hand how important this was as they were on the&nbsp;
other side of the coin not too long ago. Before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:07.680
starting Home Depot, Bernard and Arthur were both&nbsp;
working at a hardware company called Handy Dan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:12.640
Bernard had been serving as president and Arthur&nbsp;
had been serving as CFO, but despite their high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:18.640
ranking positions, they were both fired by Sanford&nbsp;
Sigiloff, who was the CEO of the parent company.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:22.880
Sanford was notorious for putting employees under&nbsp;
extreme pressure and brutally firing dozens of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:28.320
employees at a time. He was even nicknamed Ming&nbsp;
the Merciless, so Arthur and Bernard were well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:33.200
aware of how it felt to have a douchebag boss, and&nbsp;
they were determined to avoid a similar path. To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:37.920
this day, employee satisfaction is one of the&nbsp;
key advantages Home Depot has against Walmart.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:43.680
Home Depot currently has an A- company culture&nbsp;
score while Walmart has a C-. Despite such great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:48.880
core values though, the company still wasn’t doing&nbsp;
all that well in the 1970s. Home Depot ended their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:54.720
first year in business with only $81,700 in sales&nbsp;
and that was from running three stores. But it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:58.640
just a matter of time until these investments into&nbsp;
their customers and employees started to pay off.

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:02.480
MASTERING ONE INDUSTRY:&nbsp;

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:06.400
By this point, it was clear that simply offering&nbsp;
a great customer experience and convenience wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:10.560
enough to attract large numbers of customers.&nbsp;
Retail giants like Lowe’s were starting to place a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:15.120
greater emphasis on home improvement which simply&nbsp;
made it even harder to compete. Home Depot needed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:19.680
something extra to stick out and they would hit&nbsp;
the jackpot when they started hiring tradespeople.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:24.880
Home Depot started hiring full time electricians,&nbsp;
plumbers, carpenters, and painters. These guys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:28.960
would stand around their respective departments&nbsp;
and answer specific customer questions. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:33.280
example, what’s the difference between a breaker&nbsp;
and a fuse, when are you supposed to use primer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:37.440
how do you fix a clogged drain, and so on and so&nbsp;
forth. Home Depot encouraged customers to call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:42.160
the store if they had any questions, and many&nbsp;
stores even ran instructional workshops. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:46.640
made Home Depot the go to place if you wanted&nbsp;
to ask questions or get advice. And given that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:50.800
most homeowners are noobs or amateurs at home&nbsp;
improvement, this attracted a lot of customers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:58.000
Within the first half of the 1980s, Home Depot's&nbsp;
sales exploded from $81,700 in 1979 to $256&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:03.120
million in 1984. And, now that Home Depot had&nbsp;
serious sales, suppliers started taking them much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:08.080
more seriously. Home Depot was able to negotiate&nbsp;
rock bottom prices and they passed on these rock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:12.880
bottom prices to their customers. So, not only&nbsp;
were customers able to get super low prices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:17.387
but they were able to get knowledgeable customer&nbsp;
support for free. Shopping at Home Depot was a no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.387 --> 00:06:22.320
brainer for most customers, but it wasn’t all that&nbsp;
rosey from the back end. Hiring this many experts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:26.800
while maintaining razor thin profit margins was&nbsp;
super expensive. The company went public on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:32.720
NASDAQ in September of 1981 and this helped them&nbsp;
raise $4.093 million, but their debt was much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:38.560
larger than just a couple million dollars and it&nbsp;
was growing exponentially. Between 1983 and 1985,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:43.280
the company’s long term debt skyrocketed from a&nbsp;
manageable $4 million to a scary $200 million.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:49.520
This made investors extremely nervous and many&nbsp;
of them left in droves. By 1986, Home Depot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:55.040
stock had sold off 67%. Nonetheless, Home Depot&nbsp;
continued on their relentless expansion effort.

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:58.880
THRIVING AGAINST AMAZON:&nbsp;

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:03.280
In 1989, Home Depot would successfully overtake&nbsp;
Lowe’s in sales which made them the largest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:07.760
home-improvement chain in the US. But, their&nbsp;
biggest threat hadn’t even been founded yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:12.080
and that’s of course Amazon. There’s no doubt&nbsp;
that physical retailers have gotten destroyed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:16.560
over the past 20 years due to the rise of&nbsp;
online sales. But, not only has Home Depot not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:21.040
been hurt by onlines sales, but they’ve actually&nbsp;
thrived in the online environment. You see, when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:24.800
it comes to home improvement items, there’s&nbsp;
often a sense of urgency with each purchase.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:29.840
Maybe you’re mounting some cabinets and run out of&nbsp;
screws or maybe you cut a piece of wood too small.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:34.720
Most people don’t want to wait 2 days&nbsp;
or even 1 day for Amazon prime shipping.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:38.880
Their first instinct is to run to Home Depot so&nbsp;
they can get back to work as soon as possible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:43.520
And given that 90% of the US lives within 10 miles&nbsp;
of a Home Depot, running to Home Depot is a far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:47.600
better option than waiting for an Amazon delivery.&nbsp;
This sense of urgency has created a natural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:51.920
advantage for Home Depot’s physical locations, but&nbsp;
Home Depot has done much more than simply rely on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.920 --> 00:07:56.560
this advantage. Home Depot has transformed&nbsp;
their stores into a hub for online shopping.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.560 --> 00:08:00.160
For example, if you look up what you need on&nbsp;
their website, you won't have to spend any time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:04.080
searching for the product. Not only does their&nbsp;
website tell you what aisle a product is on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:08.080
but it’ll tell you what bay it’s on. They even&nbsp;
have an option to text you the location of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:12.160
product so that you don’t have to look it up&nbsp;
again in the store. You can also choose store&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:16.800
pickup which makes the entire process even easier.&nbsp;
Most of the well performing retailers like Best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:20.960
Buy and Walmart have adopted similar models,&nbsp;
but this has proven especially effective for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:25.520
Home Depot. And that’s just the buying process,&nbsp;
when it comes to home improvement, there’s a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:29.920
of returning because you either bought the wrong&nbsp;
thing or you bought too many. And I think we’d all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:34.080
agree that most of us don’t wanna sit around&nbsp;
shipping stuff back to Amazon. By this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:38.240
Home Depot had stores, customers, and revenue.&nbsp;
The only thing left to do was rake in the profit.

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:39.840
REAPING THE REWARDS:&nbsp;

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:47.680
Over the past ten years, Home Depot has basically&nbsp;
shut down their expansion efforts. Their global&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:52.480
store count has barely moved over the past&nbsp;
decade, but their net margin has gone to the moon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:56.960
Since the 2008 financial crisis to today, Home&nbsp;
Depot’s net margin has boomed from just 3-4% to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.960 --> 00:09:02.000
solid 10%. To put that in perspective, Walmart’s&nbsp;
net margin is currently at just above 1%.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:06.800
To make things even better, Home Depot’s revenue&nbsp;
has also boomed over the past year and half from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:12.880
$112 billion to $147 billion. It looks like being&nbsp;
stuck at home has resulted in a lot more demand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:17.760
for home improvement items. I’m sure skyrocketing&nbsp;
lumber prices have just helped the situation even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:22.640
further. All of this growth has pumped Home&nbsp;
Depot stock to above $400 which comes out to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:28.240
over a 1.3 million percent lifetime return. That’s&nbsp;
nearly 7 times Apple’s lifetime stock returns.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:33.120
At the end of the day, while Home Depot overtaking&nbsp;
Walmart may be surprising at first glance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:37.360
when you take a look at Home Depot’s fundamentals&nbsp;
and compare it against Walmart’s, it makes perfect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:42.640
sense. Not only is Home Depot cheap and ubiquitous&nbsp;
like Walmart, but they also provide a combination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:46.880
of knowledge, expertise, and convenience that you&nbsp;
just can’t find in any other singular location.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:50.960
And as long as Home Depot upholds their&nbsp;
excellent service, they’ll probably just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:56.000
continue to outperform Walmart. Do you guys prefer&nbsp;
Home Depot or Lowes? Comment that down below.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:59.840
Also, drop a like if you’re glad to see other&nbsp;
retailers challenging Walmart’s dominance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

